@@@@ open-backpressure
situation: open
topic: Backpressure
voice: plain
level: medium
expect: {"must":["TCP","queue"],"askMustNot":["120,?000","150 s","grows without"]}
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

Backpressure is how a slow stage tells the faster stages feeding it to slow down, instead of letting work pile up until memory runs out or every request waits behind a long queue. TCP does it with the receive window, Kafka consumers do it by pulling at their own pace, and gRPC streaming and Reactive Streams build it into the API. Without it, one slow service turns into timeouts and retries in its callers, which is how a local slowdown becomes an outage across a system. It leads on to load shedding, rate limiting and choosing queue sizes.

<ask type="predict">A producer puts 1,000 messages a second on an unbounded in-memory queue. The consumer handles 800 a second. Nothing crashes. Describe the queue's length and the wait of a newly added message after ten minutes, with rough figures.</ask>
@@ verify
# the question's answer is not stated; check the figures it turns on so the expectations are right
backlog = (1000 - 800) * 600
assert backlog == 120000 and backlog / 800 == 150
assert '120' not in reply and '150' not in reply

