@@@@ open-refresh-sharding-scan
situation: open
topic: Sharding: range versus hash partitioning
options: {"refresh":true}
voice: plain
level: easy
expect: {"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"must":["Problem 1"],"mustNot":["Why this matters"],"askMustNot":["all 8","every shard"]}
@@ reply
**Problem 1**

A users table is split across 8 shards by user id. A query reads every user with an id from 1000 to 1999. How many shards does the query have to be sent to (a) under range partitioning, where each shard holds a block of 5,000 consecutive ids starting from 0, and (b) under hash partitioning, where a user's shard is a hash of the id modulo 8?

<solution>(a) 1 shard: ids 1000 to 1999 all lie in the first block, 0 to 4999. (b) All 8: a hash scatters consecutive ids, so the 1,000 ids land on every shard (about 125 each) and the query goes to all of them.</solution>
<check lang="python">
import hashlib
ids = range(1000, 2000)
assert {i // 5000 for i in ids} == {0}
shards = {int(hashlib.sha256(str(i).encode()).hexdigest(), 16) % 8 for i in ids}
assert len(shards) == 8
</check>

<ask type="problem">The two counts, with a line of reasoning for each.</ask>
@@ verify
import hashlib, collections
ids = range(1000, 2000)
assert len(ids) == 1000 and {i // 5000 for i in ids} == {0}
for algo in ('sha256', 'md5', 'sha1'):
    c = collections.Counter(int(hashlib.new(algo, str(i).encode()).hexdigest(), 16) % 8 for i in ids)
    assert len(c) == 8 and 90 < min(c.values()) and max(c.values()) < 160, c
assert 1000 / 8 == 125 and '125' in solution(reply)
assert 'All 8' in solution(reply) and 'All 8' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ open-prior-gossip-rounds
situation: open-prior
topic: Gossip protocols and convergence rounds
voice: plain
level: medium
before: {"prior":[{"topic":"Gossip protocols and convergence rounds","daysAgo":4,"finished":false,"plan":["Push gossip, round by round","Why the informed count grows exponentially","Push, pull and push-pull"],"right":3,"partly":1,"wrong":1,"missed":["A cluster of 1,000 nodes gossips with fan-out 1. Roughly how many rounds until every node has the update?"],"open":"Why does pull gossip reach the last few nodes faster than push?"}]}
expect: {"must":["gossip"],"askMustNot":["doubl","log","exponential"]}
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

Gossip is how large clusters spread membership changes, failure suspicions and metadata with no coordinator: Cassandra, Redis Cluster and Consul's Serf layer all use it, and transaction relay in peer-to-peer networks works the same way. Its appeal is that the number of rounds grows very slowly with cluster size, so the same protocol serves ten nodes or ten thousand. You have the round-by-round mechanics of push gossip from last time; the size of the round count was the gap, and push against pull is still to come.

<ask type="short">One node in a cluster of 4,000 learns a new fact. Each round, every node that knows it tells one node chosen at random. Is the number of rounds until all 4,000 know it closest to 20, 400 or 4,000? Give your reason in a sentence.</ask>
@@ verify
import math, random
n = 4000
# the usual estimate for push gossip with fan-out 1, and a seeded simulation of it
est = math.log2(n) + math.log(n)
assert 19 < est < 22
def rounds(seed):
    rng = random.Random(seed)
    informed = {0}
    r = 0
    while len(informed) < n:
        for _ in range(len(informed)):
            informed.add(rng.randrange(n))
        r += 1
    return r
rs = [rounds(s) for s in range(20)]
assert all(15 <= r <= 30 for r in rs), rs       # closest to 20, far from 400
assert '4,000' in reply and '20, 400 or 4,000' in reply

@@@@ open-prior-replication-lag
situation: open-prior
topic: Replication lag and leader-follower reads
voice: plain
level: medium
before: {"prior":[{"topic":"Leader-follower replication and failover","daysAgo":6,"finished":true,"plan":["Writes go to the leader","Synchronous and asynchronous followers","Failover"],"right":5,"partly":1,"wrong":1,"missed":["A leader acknowledges a write before any asynchronous follower has it, then crashes. What happens to that write after failover?"]}]}
expect: {"must":["asynchronous","follower"],"askMustNot":["has not (yet )?(reached|arrived)","not (yet )?on (the|any|a) follower","only (on|in) the leader"]}
noverify: an opening; nothing is computed and the question's answer is not stated
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

Most databases you will run scale reads by sending them to followers: Postgres streaming replicas, MySQL replicas, MongoDB secondaries. A follower is always some distance behind the leader, and that distance, the replication lag, is what makes a user's own update vanish on reload or a list shrink between two refreshes. This rests on asynchronous replication from the failover lesson: the leader answers the client without waiting for its followers. That was the one point that slipped there, so it comes first. From it follow read-your-writes, monotonic reads and how to route reads safely.

<ask type="short">A leader replicates asynchronously. At the instant it tells a client "your write succeeded", what is guaranteed to have happened to that write, and what is not?</ask>

